00:15:51,360 --> 00:15:55,640
GAG, POL, and ENV.
These code for capsid

243
00:15:55,640 --> 00:15:58,360
and viral accessory
proteins, proteolytic

244
00:15:58,360 --> 00:16:01,100
enzymes, and envelope
proteins, respectively.

245
00:16:01,400 --> 00:16:05,040
The virus is surrounded
by a lipid bilayer, or

246
00:16:05,040 --> 00:16:07,960
envelope, that originates
from infected cells.

247
00:16:08,340 --> 00:16:12,300
Stock-like protein
GP41 spans the

248
00:16:12,300 --> 00:16:14,630
lipid envelope
to support GP120,

249
00:16:14,960 --> 00:16:19,060
the viral antigen that
binds the CD4 receptor on

250
00:16:19,060 --> 00:16:22,740
the T helper cell to
begin the HIV life cycle.

251
00:16:23,520 --> 00:16:25,060
We'll look at that in more

252
00:16:25,060 --> 00:16:26,640
detail in a couple of slides.

253
00:16:27,100 --> 00:16:30,560
Lining the inside and
helping to maintain

254
00:16:30,560 --> 00:16:34,100
the integrity of the
envelope is matrix protein

255
00:16:34,100 --> 00:16:38,720
P17, and all of this
surrounds the conical

256
00:16:38,720 --> 00:16:42,900
viral capsid, which
is composed of P24.

257
00:16:43,360 --> 00:16:47,080
The capsid houses
the RNA genome, which

258
00:16:47,080 --> 00:16:49,400
is lined with
stabilizing proteins.

259
00:16:49,850 --> 00:16:52,760
Prior to infecting
a cell, the reverse

260
00:16:52,760 --> 00:16:56,700
transcriptase, integrase,
and protease enzymes

261
00:16:56,700 --> 00:17:00,320
exist in inactive forms
within the capsid.

262
00:17:00,540 --> 00:17:02,620
On the right is a
short video that

263
